Hi,

I assume the first problem you mention is the one related to mathpiper ? 
We have demonstrated that this was in fact a problem in mathpiper, which 
has been in the meantime fixed by the maintainer of mathpiper.

The other one is with jython right ? I'm still not sure what the problem 
is, maybe having three languages (R, java and python) running in the 
same process (not counting C) makes a difference, but I don't know. I've 
already mentionned that you might want to try using the profiling 
features that come with rJava to identify the bottlenecks, have you ?

As for the question, java loads classes using a class loader.
http://java.sun.com/j2se/1.5.0/docs/api/index.html?java/lang/ClassLoader.html

rJava defines its own class loader (RJavaClassLoader) in order to cope 
with native libraries and loading jars from packages. My guess (and it 
was only a guess) is that the class loader that is being used by rJava 
might slow it down.

Romain


