If you want to improve your understanding I would suggest reading Java  
docs. MathPiper was using the wrong class loader (forcing the system  
loader) which is a bug (unless it was intentionally restricted but  
such limitation would be unnecessary). The whole point of the class  
loader hierarchy in Java is to allow the user to use different class  
loading mechanisms so hard-wiring a program to the system loader asks  
for trouble.


> My understanding was that the code worked all along and it was  
> modified to accommodate rJava and that was a surprise to me since I  
> was hoping that most jar files worked as is and did not require  
> special work to adapt to rJava.
>

