Of couse its true. We are using identical files: jython.jar and the
sympy files (these are not jar files) in both cases. There is NO
difference at all in the files we are using. Also we are not using
multiple jar files.  jython.jar is the only jar file involved.  Sympy
is written in python, not java, and is executed by jython.  Again
identical files are used.  The only difference is how they are called.
 In one case its from the command line followed by a single python
command and in the other its a series of calls (all of which are not
the reason for the slowdown as they are fast)  which set up and run
the single python command which is really slow.

If its true that rJava can run as fast as the java command line from
unpacked files then whatever is causing this in rJava would ideally be
fixed if feasible as its much more convenient to deploy a single jar
file than numerous unpacked files.   Although I appreciate knowing
about the workaround, using unpacked files is not really ideal.

