On Nov 19, 2009, at 12:06 , Edzer Pebesma wrote:

> Thanks for the answer, Simon. I still have to figure out if your  
> suggestion with the signal will work, as in our setup the Rserve  
> process runs as user rserve, and the java servlet runs in tomcat, I  
> believe under a user different from root.
>

That is entirely irrelevant since you'll be sending the signal from R  
to R so it's always the same user.


> One other question I have: do you have, or know of, a startup-script  
> (as in /etc/init.d on debian) that starts the Rserve daemon under a  
> separate user, say rserve? That would be nice to have, e.g. as part  
> of the rserve distribution. We faced some downtime after a power  
> failure; Rserve still needs manual start.
>

That is trivial - you don't need to do anything in the start script -  
just start Rserve and set gid and uid options in the config file (in  
that order!) - Rserve does the user switching automatically.

Cheers,
Simon


>
>
> Simon Urbanek wrote:
>> On Oct 26, 2009, at 7:53 AM, Edzer Pebesma wrote:
>>
>>> as I've mentioned before, we use Rserve in the INTAMAP.org project  
>>> to
>>> do spatial interpolation of data behind a java/tomcat client
>>> interface. A problem we've run into is the following: if the
>>> interpolation seems to take long (days...), one often breaks the
>>> request to the java client. This however does not terminate the R
>>> process. Does Rserve provide the possibility to control this, i.e.
>>> stop the Rserve child process, without completely terminating Rserve
>>> altogether?
>>>
>>
>> You can use sessions for long-term evaluation (see voidEvalDetach).
>> Another way is to use the server as the collector of the results form
>> children (see control commands in Rserve 0.6). Finally, to kill a
>> specific child, send it a a signal (e.g. INT if you calculation  
>> checks
>> signals or TERM if it doesn't) -- you can do that by calling
>> Sys.getpid() in the child first and then using another child instance
>> on the same machine to send the signal.
>>
>> Cheers,
>> Simon
